## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a practical, beginner-friendly end-to-end data pipeline with per-record contracts, canonical schemas, late data handling, and basic data quality reconciliation.

## Key Concepts

- Per-record contracts
- Lightweight schema registry
- Canonical schema and partitioning
- Watermarking for late data
- Data reconciliation and basic validation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lightweight per-record contract check (pseudo)
function validate(record){
  if(!record) return false;
  const has=(k)=>record[k]!==undefined && record[k]!==null;
  return has('device_id') && has('event_ts') && has('metric');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate schema drift across regions?
- How would you adjust the test plan for larger spikes in regional feeds?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical multi-tenant streaming design, data contracts, lineage, schema evolution, late data, and engine trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Per-record contracts and schema registry
- Per-record lineage in metadata store
- Late data handling and watermarks
- Idempotent upserts into Iceberg
- Tenant isolation and monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
// Contract check example (pseudo)
function validate(record, contract){
  for (const f of contract.fields){
    if (f.required && (record[f.name] === undefined || record[f.name] === null)) return false;
    // type checks omitted for brevity
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement a governance layer for schema evolution across tenants?
- What tests would you add to guard against drift in late-arriving records?
